{"preference":"A","margin":5,"reasons":"A puts the bottom line first (\"The short version\"), then uses labeled sections a board member can scan: reach, results, cost, evaluator, next steps. Each sentence carries one idea, and the final line says what to do: call the results preliminary until the randomized study reports. B reads as a promotional narrative. It opens with a rhetorical question and \"the answer is in\", and it replaces concrete figures with vague ones (\"nearly 5,000\" for 4,218). Its confident verbs (\"proves\", \"unmistakable\") leave a busy reader with nothing to act on and no sign of the main caveat. B also changes what the source says, and its closing line gives the board nothing to do.","objections":[{"candidate":"B","category":"clarity","quote":"One year on, the answer is in.","claim":"Sounds finished but tells the reader nothing. It sets up a question and announces an answer, and the evidence that follows does not support that answer.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"B","category":"clarity","quote":"nearly 5,000 students","claim":"Replaces the exact figure (4,218) with a vaguer, inflated one. A board member voting on a budget needs the real number.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"B","category":"audience_fit","quote":"a 38 percent advantage that proves the model works","claim":"Presents a headline figure as proof, with no caveat. The reader cannot tell how far to trust it. The 38 percent figure is also new and is never explained.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"B","category":"audience_fit","quote":"will confirm what the first year has already made clear.","claim":"The ending gives the board no action and no caveat. It tells them the matter is settled, so they cannot act on one read.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"B","category":"voice","quote":"The investment was modest.","claim":"Advocacy voice. It asserts a judgment on cost instead of letting the reader weigh $2.9 million.","severity":"minor"},{"candidate":"A","category":"economy","quote":"This comes from an internal pilot comparison that was not randomized, so the difference may reflect which students were selected for tutoring rather than the tutoring itself.","claim":"Long sentence with two clauses. Useful, but it could be split to read faster.","severity":"minor"},{"candidate":"A","category":"clarity","quote":"scale-score points","claim":"Jargon for a lay reader, with no hint of whether 3 points is large or small.","severity":"minor"}],"allegations":[{"candidate":"B","quote":"\"the results are encouraging and show that tutoring works.\"","claim":"Misquotes the evaluator. Candidate A gives the quote as \"the results are encouraging but do not yet show that tutoring caused the gains.\" B's version reverses its meaning."},{"candidate":"B","quote":"a 38 percent advantage","claim":"The source gives gains of 11 and 8 points, which is 3 points more, or 37.5 percent relative to 8. Calling it a 38 percent advantage frames a small absolute difference in a flattering way."},{"candidate":"B","quote":"the Corps delivered results that larger programs have struggled to match.","claim":"No comparison to other programs appears in the material. The claim looks unsupported."}],"out_of_lane_notes":"Accuracy matters more than style here. B alters the evaluator's quote, drops the non-randomized caveat and overstates the findings. That is a fidelity failure on top of the style problems. A preserves what the source says."}